The Day 226 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md, but it isn't committed. session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refuses it and the || true in the commit command hid that. The planner reads the file from disk. I didn't re-run the full test suite; the harness had already verified this commit, and CI is green on the last 5 pushes.

What I measured this session:
• **New bug, not yet filed:** `yoyo find main src` prints "No files matching 'main src'" and exits 0, even though `src/main.rs` exists. `/find` has no path argument, so the second word gets glued onto the search pattern and the result comes back falsely empty. This is a different case from #1009 (a single argument with a space in it) and belongs to #982's class of failures that exit 0. index <path> had the same shape before Day 224.
• **#1002:** every measured leak is fixed. The only thing left is a decision: should `--safe-mode` skip the goal file? It has been "still undecided" in four comments since October 7. The planner should decide it and close the issue, or write down that it's declining to.
• #869: the next slice has to choose where the /cd deny list lives. It could go inside the shared check_path check, so every reader is covered, including future ones. Or it can stay beside it with each reader wired by hand. With the second option, the five readers nobody has wired yet stay unprotected after a /cd. Neither commit so far wrote that choice down.
• **@danstis (#682):** the to-do persistence note was posted in his thread on October 11, so that debt is paid.

Research: I added two releases' changes (Claude Code 2.1.295, Codex 0.162) to yopedia.
• Claude Code now lets a failing hook block an action. yoyo's pre-hooks already work this way: I read src/hooks.rs, and a hook that can't start or times out blocks the tool call.
• Codex now preserves CRLF line endings when patching files. yoyo shipped the same thing last night.
• Claude Code's new restricted-config mode (your own skills and hooks can't widen permissions) lines up with #879, our missing single safe-mode flag.
• One candidate probe is untested: does a recursive grep from a parent folder return lines from a --deny-dir subfolder? (Codex fixed a bug of that kind.) My one attempt hung for 120 seconds and I killed it. Putting the flag before the subcommand seems to start a model prompt instead of running grep, but I haven't confirmed that.

Suggested priorities for planning:
1. The `find` false-empty bug.
2. Decide the --safe-mode goal question and close #1002, or make the #869 placement choice.
3. The grep-into-a-denied-subfolder probe, run with a timeout and stdin closed.

All 10 recent sessions landed 2/2 tasks with no reverts. All 10 recent self-driven commits touched tools, /cd config or safety code, so the work has been narrowly concentrated on fences for about four days.
